@page "/docs/registry/namespaces"
<PageTitle>Registry namespaces - Blaizio</PageTitle>

<div class="typeset typeset-docs space-y-10">
    <header class="docs-page-header space-y-2.5">
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2">
            <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight">Namespaces</h1>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Secondary">Registry</BzBadge>
            <BzBadge Variant="BadgeVariant.Outline">consumer side</BzBadge>
        </div>
        <p class="max-w-3xl">A consumer records a registry once under an <code>@("@namespace")</code>, then uses that prefix anywhere an item name goes. Two registries can both ship a <code>button</code> without their files, namespaces or install records colliding.</p>
    </header>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Recording one</h2>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/ConsumerAdd" />
        <p><b>Quote the pair in PowerShell.</b> A bare <code>@("@name")</code> is PowerShell's splatting operator, so an unquoted <code>@("@acme")=url</code> reaches the CLI as <code>=url</code> with the namespace expanded away. Wrapping the whole argument in quotes passes it through verbatim; <code>cmd</code>, bash and zsh need nothing. The same applies to a bare <code>blaizio search @("@acme")</code> - an item reference like <code>@("@acme/button")</code> survives unquoted, because the slash stops it being read as a variable name.</p>
        <p>The mapping is written to <code>blaizio.json</code> under <code>registries</code>. The value is a base URL or a local directory path, and the CLI appends <code>/&lt;item&gt;.json</code> to it, so the address has to be the folder your build wrote, not a template with a placeholder in it.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/RegistriesConfig" />
        <p>A registry that serves its items somewhere else records a template instead. Place <code>@("{name}")</code> where the item goes and the CLI fills it in per request; the catalogue is the same address with the reserved name <code>index</code>. Add <code>@("{style}")</code> when the registry publishes one variant per skin, and it takes the project's recorded style.</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/Template" />
        <p>A registry that needs a token is recorded the same way, with the headers or parameters its requests carry: see <a href="docs/registry/authentication">Authentication</a>. Its entry becomes an object instead of a string, and the plain ones beside it stay as they are.</p>
        <p>Two names are refused. <code>@("@default")</code> already means the project's own registry (see <a href="#depending-on-the-base-set">Depending on the base set</a>), and <code>@("@blaizio")</code> would put its installs in a <code>Blaizio</code> folder, whose namespace segment hides the <code>Blaizio</code> packages from every file inside it: the components would land and then fail to compile.</p>
        <p><CmdChip Command="blaizio registry list" /> prints what is recorded, and <CmdChip Command="blaizio registry remove" /> drops one. Removing a registry forgets the source only: components already installed from it stay exactly where they are, and <CmdChip Command="blaizio remove" /> is what uninstalls them.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Where the files land</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Their own folder.</b> Files from <code>@("@acme")</code> go to <code>Components/Ui/Acme/</code> instead of mixing into the top level.</li>
            <li><b>Their own namespace segment.</b> The components are rewritten to <code>MyApp.Components.Ui.Acme</code>, so a name that exists in two registries still compiles.</li>
            <li><b>Their own record.</b> <code>blaizio.json</code> tracks the install as <code>@("@acme/tag")</code>, which is what lets <CmdChip Command="blaizio update" />, <code>add --diff</code> and <CmdChip Command="blaizio remove" /> work per registry.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">How a reference resolves</h2>
        <p>Anywhere an item is named, on the command line or inside another item's <code>registryDependencies</code>, the CLI reads the reference the same way:</p>
        <BzTable ContainerClass="rounded-lg border" Class="text-sm">
            <BzTableHeader>
                <BzTableRow Class="bg-muted/50">
                    <BzTableHead>Reference</BzTableHead>
                    <BzTableHead>Resolves to</BzTableHead>
                </BzTableRow>
            </BzTableHeader>
            <BzTableBody Class="text-muted-foreground [&_code]:text-foreground">
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>button</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The registry configured as the project's default, or whatever <code>--registry</code> overrides it with.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>@("@acme/tag")</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The registry recorded for <code>@("@acme")</code>, at its default layout or through its template. An unrecorded namespace is looked up in the <a href="docs/registry/directory">community directory</a> - a hit is offered and recorded on acceptance; a namespace listed nowhere is an error naming the command that records it.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>@("@default/button")</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>The project's own default registry, named explicitly. Reserved, so it is never recorded and never a real namespace - see <a href="#depending-on-the-base-set">Depending on the base set</a>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>https://.../tag.json</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>That exact document. From a host the project has not already chosen, this warns and asks first.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>acme/toolkit/tag</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>An item in a public repository, optionally pinned with <code>#ref</code>. See <a href="docs/registry/repositories">Repositories</a>.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
                <BzTableRow><BzTableCell Class="whitespace-nowrap"><code>./local/tag.json</code></BzTableCell><BzTableCell>A file on disk, which is how you test an item before it is hosted anywhere.</BzTableCell></BzTableRow>
            </BzTableBody>
        </BzTable>
        <p>Plain names are normalized before they become a URL: <code>InputNumber</code>, <code>inputnumber</code> and <code>Input-Number</code> all reach <code>input-number</code>, matched against the registry's catalogue when it has one. Item names never become path segments beyond that, so a name carrying a slash or <code>..</code> is rejected rather than fetched.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="depending-on-the-base-set">
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Depending on the base set</h2>
        <p><b>Inside a namespaced item, a plain dependency name means that item's own registry.</b> Under <code>@("@acme/tag")</code>, a <code>registryDependencies</code> entry of <code>chip</code> resolves to <code>@("@acme/chip")</code> - which is what you want for a registry that ships a complete set, and what stops two registries' same-named components colliding.</p>
        <p>It is <em>not</em> what you want when your component builds on the base components the consumer already has. An editor that needs <code>toolbar</code> and <code>select</code> means the ones in the consumer's registry, not copies it would have to ship and keep in step. Declaring them plainly makes the CLI look for <code>@("@acme/toolbar")</code> and fail with <em>"Registry file not found"</em>, pointing at your own registry.</p>
        <p>Name them with the reserved <code>@("@default")</code> namespace instead. It always means <b>the consumer's default registry</b> - theirs, not yours, so a project pointed at a mirror or a fork still resolves against its own:</p>
        <CodeBlock Snippet="Registry/DefaultDependency" />
        <p>Both forms in one item: <code>toolbar</code> and <code>select</code> come from the consumer's registry, <code>chip</code> from yours. Such a dependency installs exactly as the consumer's own copy would: the ordinary output folder, the ordinary namespace, and an install record under its plain name - no <code>Acme/</code> subfolder, no duplicate component. If it is already installed, it is simply reused.</p>
        <p><code>@("@default")</code> cannot be recorded as a namespace (<CmdChip Command="blaizio registry add" /> rejects it) and works on the command line too, where <code>blaizio add @("@default/button")</code> is just an explicit way of writing <code>blaizio add button</code>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 data-toc class="scroll-mt-20">Searching across registries</h2>
        <p><CmdChip Command="blaizio search" /> takes namespaces, URLs or local paths, several at a time, and falls back to the project's default registry when given none. The query, type and page travel to each registry, and one that can answer server-side does - the full contract, on both sides, is on the <a href="docs/registry/search">Search</a> page.</p>
    </section>
</div>
